WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.360
New Zealand’s Nelson Tasman 
region is the home of sunny beaches, 

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.280
outrageous landscapes and Nelson,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.360
a small city that boasts a thriving art scene, 

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.800
craft breweries and wineries, 

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.760
and a farmer's market famed for its local specialties. 

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.600
National Geographic sent my colleagues and me
to explore the area to tell it’s story. 

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.800
Photographer Erika Larsen visited the beautiful 
Abel Tasman national park

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.320
to take a hike and a kayak trip with local guide, Lee-Anne Jago.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.400
Kia Ora, ngā mihi.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.520
Welcome to the Abel Tasman.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.720
Thank you, thanks for having me.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.720
I wanted to meet you here, because of this beautiful view, 
looking over Te Pukatea.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.800
This is where we are going to start our kayaking today.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.840
It’s amazing, the colors are beautiful.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.280
We've got a granite coastline,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.000
and in granite there's iron and so the iron corrodes with 
salt water and creates the orangey colour.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:25.320
It’s really accessible for all kinds of people, 
you don’t have to be really fit and climb mountains.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.360
People can kayak or you can walk. 

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.560
We've got beautiful campsites right along the Abel Tasman. 

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.200
So it’s a park   that you can really take it 
at your own pace, whatever that is.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.440
Whatever that is, yeah.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.400
We have a word in our culture, manaakitanga,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.840
and that means, how we look after people. 

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:52.960
So it gives us great mana, it uplifts us 
when we look after people in the best possible way.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.520
Alright, let’s go kayaking.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.880
Yes, I can’t wait!

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.200
[laughs]

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.200
So this is split apple rock, 
tokangawhā is the Māori name.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.040
Toka means rock, and ngawhā means to burst, or split.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.440
We know that four hundred years ago
the rock was already split, 

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.960
but at one stage it would have been a complete boulder. 

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.040
Everyone entering the Abel Tasman 
generally will come by here. 

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.520
Ah so it’s that well known.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.320
It’s like the gateway.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.280
Just over an hour away from Abel Tasman,

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:52.320
Just over an hour away from Abel Tasman,
I went to explore Nelson’s famous market,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.120
I went to explore Nelson’s famous market,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.440
and what I found blew me away.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.000
Why hello! This looks fantastic.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.160
We’re passionate about real ingredients, 
and the health benefits of that.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.360
Oh wow, that one’s really good.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.600
Yeah, it’s a bit addictive.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.520
Triple cream Brie, 
with a line of shaved black truffle.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.600
Oh my God.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.080
Crispy outside, inside is still moist and warm.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:36.680
Okay!

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.280
And they make you very happy temporarily.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.760
[laughs]

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.280
I’m here with Pic, from Pic’s Peanut Butter,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.920
and Pic, I want to learn all about why
this peanut butter is so special.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.080
I keep hearing all about it.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.560
Well, I didn’t realise it was special, 

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.240
I just made some peanut butter because I got cross 
with buying peanut butter that was full of sugar, 

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.760
and I made peanut butter at home and it tasted nice.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.400
You actually started selling it here, at this market.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.280
Yeah, but it wasn’t until I took it  overseas, 

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.240
to America that I realised that it was um, that it was special. 

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.400
I can tell that this is a community that likes to start things.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.840
It is, it’s a wonderful market.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.560
I know that for a lot of people New Zealand 
is high on the bucket list.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.840
Yeah. No a lot of people say, 
they say that New Zealand's on my bucket list,

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.320
and I say look, there's no point 
in having any other list just get down here, 

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.120
because I know they're going to love it. 

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.560
I don’t know why the world doesn’t live here.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:27.960
- [laughs]
- You know?

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.800
Meanwhile near Nelson, artist and author Christoph Niemann

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.680
visited renowned local potter Katie Gold at her home studio.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.120
- Hi Katie
- Hi

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:47.880
- Nice to meet you
- Hi

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.000
Nice to meet you too.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.880
So we’re at you home, and your work place and your gallery.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.080
Well we live here and work here, work from home my husband and I. 

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.520
I use slabs of clay and I build things up, make mostly vessels.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.840
Tell me a little about the process.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.920
A lot of people have seen more work before,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.720
they're wanting something representing 
part of New Zealand, where they live.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.920
We look at other pieces I’ve made and 
then we turn that into a piece of work for them.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.880
One’s like this I have put the map of New Zealand. 
So this is the old Captain Cook's map.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.200
- It's been printed on.
- Oh, okay.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.440
So this is when New Zealand was spelt without an ‘a’. 

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.600
This piece here, the Pōhutukawa, which is 
the New Zealand Christmas tree.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.040
That flowers every December in New Zealand.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.720
Would you like to come see where I make all the work.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.440
- I would love to see the studio.
- Okay, great!

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.720
Take a little bit of clay, roll it into a ball.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:47.400
And then you pop it into a little mold, and pop it out 
and you have the shell. 

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.640
So with all the shells, 

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:53.920
I then turn them into these little bowls 
and these can hang on the wall as well.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.000
This is for an auction,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.080
but it’s got all the paper roads and the maps and the flaxes 
and what you’ll see on the coast in Nelson.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.680
Yeah from our trip, we felt a lot that it was about 
the connection from land to sea,

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.080
and it’s beautiful how it all comes together again, 
in the sculpture.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.000
So the Nelson region is full of creative people 
and we love sharing it with visitors who come here,

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.920
especially if they give it a go as well.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.360
This has been a huge privilege.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.960
Our journey ended at Neudorf Vineyards where we met Rosie Finn.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.080
Now this is nice.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.440
[laughs]

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.240
So mum and dad still live on the property,

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.440
their house is just on the other side of those buildings. 

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.520
They planted the vineyard in 1978 
and did their first vintage in 1981.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.960
These are the vineyards that you parents planted?

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.040
Yep, this is all of the original planting, so some of these,

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.240
these are some of the oldest Chardonnay vines
in the South Island. 

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.560
There's a lot of respect for the soil and the land, 
and where we are.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.880
It seems like Nelson is the kind of place that has lots of crafts 
and artisans, am I right about that?

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.720
Yeah, absolutely I think Nelson’s always had 
an abundance of creativity. 

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.960
This area in particular you've got potters, cider makers 
and olive oil and there's a lot of inspiration in the land.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.240
You’ve got the Abel Tasman this way
and the Nelson lakes that way.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:23.400
So it’s one of those areas where it’s 
pretty hard not to be inspired I think.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:24.960
Right, cheers to that!

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.160
[laughs]

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.200
[glasses clink]

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.520
The Nelson Tasman region is truly magical, 

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.400
the people we met,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.840
the warm welcomes we received, 

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.480
the natural beauty we discovered.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.960
It’s a place to find inspiration.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.480
A place where art and craftsmanship, natural wonder

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.000
and food and drink

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.800
come together to create a culture of personal connection.

